        "C is the correct answer. A and B are incorrect as they describe the effects of glucagon on glycogen synthesis/breakdown but do not mention the effects of insulin. D and E are incorrect as they describe the effects of insulin on glycogen synthesis/breakdown but do not mention the effects of glucagon.  The effects of glucagon and insulin on glycogen synthesis/breakdown are as follows:-Glucagon (and adrenaline) acts on hepatocytes in the following steps:-activates adenylate cyclase-large increase in cAMP-activation of cAMP dependent protein kinase A-phosphorylation of many proteins, including phosphorylase kinase = activation-phosphorylation of glycogen synthase = deactivation.-Phosphorylase kinase phosphorylates glycogen phosphorylase-b to make active glycogen phosphorylase-a-Enhances its activity, causing glycogen breakdown.-Net result=extremely large induction of glycogen breakdown in response to glucagon  Insulin acts on hepatocytes in the following steps:-activates phosphodiesterase-large increase in AMP from cAMP-no activation of cAMP dependent protein kinase A-activation of phosphatases (phosphoprotein phosphatase 1)-phosphorylation of many proteins, including phosphorylase kinase = deactivation-phosphorylation of glycogen synthase = activation.-Phosphoprotein phosphatase phosphorylates glycogen phosphorylase-a to make inactive glycogen phosphorylase-b-Inhibits its activity, preventing glycogen breakdown.-Net result= end glycogenolysis in order to preserve what glycogen stores are left and trigger glycogenesis",
        "The correct answer is B! Theca granulosa cells secrete androgens which are then converted to oestrogen by the follicular granulosa cells.",
        "The thalamus is a relay station for sensory and motor information. It is involved in consciousness, selective attention and memory. The limbic system is involved in emotional response.",
        "Bisphosphonates decrease bone deposition by osteoblasts and increase absorption by osteoclasts.Strontium increases bone deposition by osteoblasts and reduces absorption by osteoclasts.Raloxifene is a selective oestrogen receptor modulator that increases bone deposition by osteoblasts and reduces absorption by osteoclasts.Teriparatide is a parathyroid hormone analogue that increases bone deposition by osteoblasts and reduces absorption by osteoclasts.",
        "The oesophagus passes through the diaphragm at T10.",
        "The correct answer is B! The risk factors for sudden cardiac death in patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy are: 1. Family history of sudden cardiac death in a first degree relative2. Symptoms of ventricular tachycardia3. Septal thickness of > 30mm  This patient has 3 risk factors, and therefore is classed as a high risk patient.",
        "C is the correct answer! PCT= Transport with organic molecules (e.g glucose)Thick ascending limb= Na+/K+2Cl- transporter DCT= Na+/Cl- co-transporter  PCT= Na+ is reabsorbed via transport with organic molecules (e.g glucose)Thick ascending limb= Na+/K+2Cl- transporter DCT= Na+/Cl- co-transporter",
        "PLA2 is the correct answer. PLA2 is an enzyme that cleaves fatty acids from membrane phospholipids.  PLA1 and PLA2 are both phospholipases, but PLA1 cleaves fatty acids from the sn-1 position of the phospholipid, while PLA2 cleaves fatty acids from the sn-2 position.  PLB1 and PLB2 are also phospholipases, but they cleave fatty acids from the sn-3 position of the phospholipid.  Phospholipase C is also a phospholipase, but it cleaves the phosphodiester bond in the middle of the phospholipid, producing two smaller molecules.",
        "A is a description of symportersB is a description of uniportersC is a description of antiportersD is a description of ABC transporters",
        "Aspirin and ibuprofen are non steroidal anti inflammatory substances that block cyclo-oxgenase enzymes, to reduce the production of prostaglandins.",
        "The foramen ovale is a flap in the wall between the right and left atria. In foetuses, the right atrium has higher pressure than the left atrium, so blood flows from the right atrium to the left atrium through the foramen ovale. At birth, the pressure in the left atrium is higher than in the right atrium, so blood flows from the left atrium to the right atrium. This causes the foramen ovale to close.",
        "C is the correct answer! A - PRI is the time between the P wave and the QRS complex. A prolonged PRI is indicative of an AV block.B - Regular-irregular rhythm is indicative of atrial fibrillation.D - Regular rhythm with flutters is indicative of atrial flutter.E - QRS drop is indicative of a bundle branch block.",
        "Dane particles are a type of virus found in the blood of people with Hepatitis B infection.",
        "The correct answer is C! This is a classic example of how to differentiate between the different types of meningitis.  A - Bacterial meningitis (e.g. N. meningitidis) - CSF is usually turbid, PMNL is high, lymphocytes are low, protein is high, glucose is lowB - Viral meningitis (e.g. Herpes Simplex 1) - CSF is usually clear, PMNL is low, lymphocytes are high, protein is low, glucose is similar to plasmaD - TB meningitis - CSF is usually clear, PMNL is low, lymphocytes are high, protein is high, glucose is lowE - Neisseria Meningitidis - Same as A",
        "The correct answer is E! The patient is showing signs of pericarditis. The symptoms are: - Chest pain that is stabbing and central - Pain is worse when lying down and bending forward - Malaise - Shortness of breath The ECG findings are: - ST segment elevation - PR depression The echocardiogram is abnormal because the pericardium is thickened and the heart is pushed into the pleural cavity. The viral serology is negative because the patient has not had a viral infection. Aortic dissection is a tear in the wall of the aorta. It is a medical emergency. The symptoms are: - Sudden onset of severe, tearing chest pain - Pain is worse when leaning forward - Pain radiates to the back - Blood pressure is high on one side of the body - The patient may have a pulse deficit - The patient may have a widened pulse pressure  Bronchiectasis is a condition where the airways in the lungs become damaged and widened. This causes mucus to build up in the airways and makes it difficult to breathe. The symptoms are: - Shortness of breath - Coughing up mucus - Wheezing - Chest pain  Unstable angina is a type of chest pain that is caused by a blockage in the coronary arteries. The symptoms are: - Chest",
        "IgG is the only antibody to cross the placenta and therefore is found in high concentrations in newborns.",
