        "E is correct! The oculomotor nerve innervates the parasympathetic fibres of the pupillary constrictor and the ciliary muscle. The facial nerve innervates the parasympathetic fibres of the lacrimal gland and the submandibular and sublingual salivary glands. The glossopharyngeal nerve innervates the parasympathetic fibres of the parotid salivary gland. The vagus nerve innervates the parasympathetic fibres of the thoracic and abdominal viscera.",
        "The correct answer is C! The uterine artery is the artery that must be ligated during a hysterectomy. It arises from the internal iliac artery. The phrase 'water under the bridge' is a mnemonic to remember the position of the ureter relative to the uterine artery.",
        "Amiodarone is the most likely culprit for the patient's development of torsade de Pointes. Amiodarone is a Class III anti-arrhythmic drug which is used to treat ventricular arrhythmias. It is contraindicated in patients with a long QT interval as it can prolong the QT interval further and increase the risk of torsade de Pointes.  Adenosine is a Class I anti-arrhythmic drug which is used to treat supraventricular tachycardias. It is contraindicated in patients with a long QT interval as it can prolong the QT interval further and increase the risk of torsade de Pointes.  Digoxin is a Class III anti-arrhythmic drug which is used to treat atrial fibrillation. It is contraindicated in patients with a long QT interval as it can prolong the QT interval further and increase the risk of torsade de Pointes.  Metroprolol is a beta-blocker which is used to treat angina and heart failure. It is contraindicated in patients with a long QT interval as it can prolong the QT interval further and increase the risk of torsade de Pointes.  Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker which is used to treat angina and supraventricular tachycardias. It is contraindicated in patients with a long QT interval as it can prolong the QT interval further and increase the risk of torsade de Pointes.",
